Sean,

You have already done so in your answer. For procD.lm, use of summary() or better anova() will generate the anova table.  The probability is the last element of the table you provided.

Then, if you wish to extract that, one has myriad options in R.  One is to do res <- anova(mod)    Then extract the component you wish from the resultant object.

I note that these are not geomorph-specific questions but rather general R-use questions. Reading a basic book on R: such as ‘An Introduction to R’, which is found freely on-line and producte by the R-Core Team, would be strongly advised. This will help with general syntax of how to use R, which in turn will assist with use of geomorph.

